Title:
Malware Just Got Its Free Passes Back! (Moonwalk++ / StackMoonwalk++)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Follow-up research to DEFCON 31 “StackMoonwalk” showing how to extend stack moonwalking beyond basic call stack desynchronization to evade modern call-stack-telemetry detections.  
- It analyzes how EDRs (notably Elastic’s call stack enrichment model inspired by the Eclipse algorithm) label suspicious stacks (proxy calls, ROP, shellcode, unbacked RWX, truncated stacks) and which assumptions those detections rely on.  
- The post introduces a PoC (“Moonwalk++”, published as Moonwalk--) that crafts fully unwindable, logically valid spoofed frame sequences while bypassing checks like “instruction before return address must be a CALL” and gadget/ROP heuristics.  
- It further demonstrates hiding the true caller and eliminating RWX/RX indicators by relocating initial frames into a legitimate image, dynamically resolving thread start, and using concealed ROP chains to encrypt/decrypt payload memory and flip protections around API execution.  
- Public tools relying on call stack analysis (e.g., Hunt-Sleeping-Beacons, Get-InjectedThreadEx, hollows_hunter in stack-based modes) are shown to miss the tampering, highlighting limits of stack-only provenance.  
- Useful for red teamers/malware developers building stealthy usermode execution, and for blue teamers validating robustness of call-stack-based detections.

Technical Focus:
- Windows x64 stack spoofing with unwindable frames (UNWIND_INFO, RtlVirtualUnwind)
- Eclipse-style call-site validation and bypass strategies
- ROP/JOP/COP gadget selection and “padding” instructions to preserve semantics
- Remote thread execution and thread start address legitimacy (BaseThreadInitThunk context)
- In-memory payload encryption/decryption + VirtualProtect-style permission flipping to avoid RWX/RX artifacts

Use Cases:
- Evading EDR call-stack provenance for sensitive Win32/NTAPI calls
- Building stealthier injected shellcode that maintains plausible unwindable stacks
- Hiding sleeping-beacon style DelayExecution stacks from stack-based hunters
- Researching/benchmarking defensive call stack validation logic and its failure modes
- Developing detections that go beyond stack metadata (cross-checking call targets, memory maps, and execution transitions)

Keywords:
StackMoonwalk, Moonwalk++, FullMoon, Eclipse algorithm, call stack telemetry, stack spoofing, RtlVirtualUnwind, UNWIND_INFO, UWOP_SET_FPREG, UWOP_PUSH_NONVOL, UWOP_ALLOC_SMALL, ROP, JOP, COP, stack pivot, BaseThreadInitThunk, VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory, CreateRemoteThread, VirtualProtect, SystemFunction032, Elastic Security Labs, Hunt-Sleeping-Beacons, hollows_hunter, Get-InjectedThreadEx, OneDrive.exe injection